<<~ ahu #/shelf>>

! Wikitext paint — the grammar research shelf

''OPERATOR RULING, 2026-09-11.'' The base `tiddlywiki` wikitext grammar aligns to this research
BEFORE any `memetic-wikitext` refactor stands on top of it. Current integrations open to radical
rethinking as the base heals to this spec. Builds implement THESE findings, never improvisation.

''WHY THIS SHELF EXISTS.'' A research swarm ran four spirits, and those spirits ran four
sub-spirits of their own. Each synthesis compressed its critics out: primary evidence reached the
parent as a verdict, and the verdict reached the house without the measurement under it. The
sub-spirits' findings stand here in their own words — quotes, URLs, counts, and every
NOT-VERIFIED flag they raised. ''A synthesis keeps what it agrees with; a shelf keeps what it
measured.''

!! What the swarm answered

|!# |!question |!verdict |
|1 |Does canon split a multi-character delimiter into two named tokens? |NO GRAMMAR DOES |
|2 |How does a delimiter take its content's colour? |MULTI-SCOPE STACKING, never a compound root |
|3 |Which family names a call's argument, and its `=`? |SURFACE decides, and they part correctly |
|4 |Does `meta.*` bless an unbounded interpolated vocabulary? |MEASURED PRACTICE, html does it 79 times |
|5 |Do 453 names and 46% punctuation tokens read large? |ORDINARY — C++ carries 471 and 31.8% |
|6 |How do wiki grammars treat their brackets? |FOUR ANSWERS across seven grammars, no convergence |
|7 |May an extension ship a calm default? |LEGAL, 450 precedents, and NOT YOURS TO HAVE |
|8 |Does "one unit, one colour" carry a named principle? |BRINGHURST 3.5.1, verbatim |

---

! I — THE CANON, with its evidence kept

!! 1 · Per-glyph delimiter tokens

''MEASURED over 13 fetched grammars: NO grammar splits a multi-character delimiter into two
separately-named sequential tokens.'' The universal idiom gives one capture to the whole
delimiter, however many characters it holds.

|!grammar |!delimiter |!how scoped |!split? |
|markdown |`**` |capture 1 -> `punctuation.definition.bold.markdown` |one token |
|markdown |```` ``` ```` |capture 3 -> `punctuation.definition.markdown` |one token, any length |
|html |`</` |`"end": "(?i)(</)(\2)\s*(>)"`, cap 1 -> `punctuation.definition.tag.begin.html` |''ONE token'' |
|Vue |`{{` / `}}` |cap 1 -> `punctuation.definition.interpolation.begin.html.vue` |one token each |
|TypeScript |`${` |capture 0 -> `punctuation.definition.template-expression.begin.ts` |one token |
|wikitext |`[[` |cap 2 -> `punctuation.definition.tag.link.internal.begin.wikitext` |one token |
|Twig |`{{` |cap 0 -> `punctuation.section.tag.twig` |one token, incl. the `-`/`~` modifier |
|Liquid |`{%-` |cap 1 -> `punctuation.definition.tag.begin.liquid` |''one token, 3 chars'' |
|Handlebars |`{{~{>` |cap 1 -> `support.constant.handlebars` |''one token, arbitrary length'' |
|Rust |`println!` |the `!` sits INSIDE the name capture |not split |
|LaTeX |`\usepackage` |cap 1 spans `\usepackage`, cap 2 spans the bare `\` |''nested, not sequential'' |

''The TextMate 1.x manual's own example FUSES a two-character delimiter'':

```
{ match = '(@selector\()(.*?)(\))'; captures = { 1 = { name = 'storage.type.objc'; }; 3 = …; }; };
```
> "In that example we match text like `@selector(windowWillClose:)` but the `storage.type.objc`
> name will only be assigned to `@selector(` and `)`."

''Sublime names delimiters as PAIRED begin/end, never per-character'':
> "Punctuation at the beginning and end of strings should use: `punctuation.definition.string.begin`
> / `punctuation.definition.string.end`"

''The VS Code syntax-highlight guide carries NO scope-naming guidance at all.'' MEASURED:
`grep -ic "naming"` -> ''0''; `grep -ic "convention"` -> ''0''. It defers to TextMate 25 times.

''The manual's 11 root groups: `comment, constant, entity, invalid, keyword, markup, meta, storage,
string, support, variable`.'' `punctuation` stands NOWHERE among them — a later Sublime and
community addition.

!! 2 · How a delimiter takes its content's colour — THE DEVICE

''A literal `string.punctuation.*` — a punctuation path under a content root — returns ZERO across
13 grammars.''

```
grep -o 'string\.punctuation[^"]*'  *.json *.yaml *.plist   -> 0
grep -o 'markup\.punctuation[^"]*'  *.json *.yaml *.plist   -> 0
grep -o 'keyword\.punctuation[^"]*' *.json *.yaml *.plist   -> 0
```

Sublime rules against it directly:
> "Avoid `entity.name.type.class` and `entity.name.type.struct` which unnecessarily nest scope
> labels under `type`."

The one grammar that DOES nest a delimiter under a content root — Jinja — stands as evidence of the
anti-pattern, not of canon: it invents `variable.entity.other.jinja.delimiter` and
`variable.meta.scope.jinja`, and gives `captures` as a JSON ARRAY instead of a capture-keyed object,
which makes those captures ''inert''.

''THE ANSWER THE SYNTHESIS DROPPED. Respected grammars give a delimiter the content's colour by
STACKING two space-separated scopes on ONE token.''

```
grep -ohE '"name": "[a-z][a-z0-9._$-]+ [^"]*"' *.json | wc -l   -> 181 total
per file: python 77 · ts 42 · tsx 42 · rust 9 · latex 6 · handlebars 5
```

''MagicPython'' — VS Code's own bundled Python grammar, 12 occurrences:
```json
"beginCaptures": {
  "2": { "name": "string.interpolated.python string.quoted.single.python storage.type.string.python" },
  "3": { "name": "punctuation.definition.string.begin.python string.quoted.single.python" }
}
```
The quote character carries ''both'' `punctuation.definition.string.begin.python` ''and''
`string.quoted.single.python`.

''VS Code TypeScript'' — the same device for backticks, and it says why:
```json
"contentName": "string.template.ts",
"beginCaptures": {
  "2": { "name": "string.template.ts punctuation.definition.string.template.begin.ts" }
}
```
Because `contentName` covers only the interior, the backtick would otherwise LOSE the string
colour — so they stack it explicitly. Also present in ts/tsx:
`"meta.block.ts punctuation.definition.block.ts"`,
`"meta.object-literal.key.ts punctuation.separator.key-value.ts"`.
''Handlebars'': `"support.constant.handlebars keyword.control"`.

A second, non-name mechanism appears in html.json — ''overlapping captures'', where capture 0 and
capture 1 cover the same closing quote:
```json
"end": "(\")",
"endCaptures": { "0": { "name": "punctuation.definition.string.end.html" },
                 "1": { "name": "source.css" } }
```

''THIS HOUSE ALREADY WRITES MULTI-SCOPE NAMES.'' The call-side widening put `meta.variable.call.*`
before the published names in one `name` string, and `vscode-textmate` returns every
space-separated scope. The device stands native here. ''The fold is reachable canonically.''

!! 3 · A call's argument name, and its separator

''Sublime, verbatim'':
> ''`variable.parameter`'' — "Parameters to a function or methods should use the following scope.
> This may also be used for other parameter-like variables, such as receivers or named return
> values in Go."
> ''`entity.other.attribute-name`'' — "HTML, CSS and XML use the following for tag attribute names"
> — and from the `entity.` preamble: "The notable exceptions are `entity.name.tag` and
> `entity.other.attribute-name`, which are used in HTML and XML tags."
> ''`keyword.operator.assignment`'' — "Operators are typically symbols, so the term `keyword` can
> seem somewhat contradictory."
> ''`punctuation.separator`'' — "Separators such as commas and colons should use:
> `punctuation.separator`"

TextMate 1.x on `variable.parameter`: "when the variable is declared as the parameter" —
''declaration-side only.'' The manual says nothing about call-site named arguments.

Both names sit in Sublime's ''Minimal Scope Coverage'' set, so any decent theme colours both.

|!grammar |!argument name |!separator |
|Python `f(x=1)` |`variable.parameter.function-call.python` |`keyword.operator.assignment.python` |
|''wikitext'' `{{T\|name=v}}` |`entity.other.attribute-name.local-name.wikitext` |`keyword.operator.equal.wikitext` |
|Handlebars |`variable.parameter.handlebars` STACKED inside `entity.other.attribute-name.handlebars` |none separate |
|TSX (JSX) |`entity.other.attribute-name.tsx` |`keyword.operator.assignment.tsx` |
|VS Code html |`entity.other.attribute-name.html` |`punctuation.separator.key-value.html` |
|Vue |`entity.other.attribute-name.html.vue` |`punctuation.separator.key-value.html.vue` |
|Liquid |`entity.other.attribute-name.liquid`, colon FUSED into the token |none |
|Twig |`variable.other.twig` |`:` -> `punctuation.separator.key-value`; `=` -> `keyword.operator.assignment` |
|Jinja |no named-arg scope at all |`keyword.operator.assignment.jinja` |

''MediaWiki's template call — the closest analogue this grammar has'':
```yaml
- match: (?<=\|)\s*(?:([-\w.]+)(:))?([-\w\s\.:]+)\s*(=)
  captures:
    3: { name: entity.other.attribute-name.local-name.wikitext }
    4: { name: keyword.operator.equal.wikitext }
```

''THE SPLIT IS BY LANGUAGE SURFACE, never by call-versus-definition.'' Tag, template and markup
surfaces — html, Vue, JSX, Liquid, Handlebars, ''wikitext templates'' — take
`entity.other.attribute-name.*`. Programming-language call surfaces — Python kwargs — take
`variable.parameter.function-call.*`. For the `=`: `keyword.operator.assignment` holds the majority
in code-flavoured grammars AND in wikitext itself; `punctuation.separator.key-value` holds the
majority for a `:`. ''Only Handlebars stacks both'', which stands as the hedge where both colours
matter.

''And TiddlyWiki's own parser marks the distinction this house nearly erased.''
`core/modules/parsers/parseutils.js:339-352`, `parseMacroParameterAsAttribute`:
```js
separatorToken = nameToken && $tw.utils.parseTokenRegExp(source,namePos,/=|:/y),
node.assignmentOperator = separatorToken.match[0];
isNewStyleSeparator = (node.assignmentOperator === "=");
```
The `=` SELECTS A VALUE GRAMMAR — it admits filtered, indirect and substituted values that a `:`
refuses. A widget attribute's `=` carries no such choice. ''The call's `=` and the widget's `=` part
correctly. The argument NAME is what wrongly parts.''

!! 4 · `meta.*`, and an unbounded interpolated vocabulary

''TextMate 1.x manual'':
> "A minimal theme will only assign styles to 10 of the 11 root groups below (''`meta` does not get
> a visual style'')…"
> "`meta` — the meta scope is generally used to markup larger parts of the document… and ''only the
> latter would be styled''. …most of the time meta scopes are however used in scope selectors for
> activation of bundle items."

''Sublime'':
> "Meta scopes are used to scope larger sections of code or markup… ''These are not intended to be
> styled by a color scheme, but used by preferences and plugins.''"
> "''When styling scopes, resist the urge to directly style `meta` scopes.''"

''The one `meta` prohibition that could bite is about STACKING, not interpolation'':
> "Each variant should be applied to a specific part, and not stacked. For example,
> `meta.function.php meta.function.parameters.php` should never occur, but instead the scopes
> should alternate between `meta.function.php` then `meta.function.parameters.php` and back."

''On capture interpolation the docs stand SILENT.'' The TM manual documents `captures` and states
"Currently `name` is the only attribute supported"; the only `$`-substitution it documents anywhere
is `include = "$self"`. Sublime's "interpolation" hits are about string interpolation in source
code. The VS Code guide has zero. ''Neither blesses it and neither forbids it'' — `$n` in a `name`
stands as a vscode-textmate extension over the 1.x spec.

''MEASURED — real grammars do it'':
```
"name" values carrying $n          TOTAL / UNIQUE
  html.json      79 / 53
  vue.json       56 /  4
  latex.json      1 /  1
  md.json         1 /  1
  ts, tsx, python, rust, handlebars, jinja, liquid, wikitext, twig   0
```

VS Code html, 52 of 53 unique interpolated names under `meta.`, and one DEMONSTRABLY UNBOUNDED —
`$1` here is any attribute name, matched by a pure exclusion class:
```json
"begin": "([^\\x{0020}\"'<>/=\\x{0000}-\\x{001F}…]+)",
"name": "meta.attribute.unrecognized.$1.html"
```

''Interpolation is NOT confined to `meta.`'' — Vue interpolates under a STYLED root with an
unbounded capture: `"name": "entity.name.tag.$1.html.vue"`, `[a-zA-Z0-9:-]+`.

''So `meta.tag.widget.$3` and `meta.variable.call.parameter.tw-$1` sit squarely inside measured
practice.'' An unrecognised name under `meta.` cannot mis-colour anything; one under `entity.` can.
The 186 `meta` names painting 0/65 read as ''the contract holding'', never as bloat.

!! 5 · Vocabulary size and token share

|!grammar |!bytes |!distinct scopes |!`punctuation.*` |!`meta.*` |!punct. share |!interpolated |
|VS Code markdown |79 877 |135 |27 |74 |20.0% |1 |
|VS Code html |84 416 |62 |14 |22 |22.6% |''53'' |
|VS Code TypeScript |231 555 |261 |44 |60 |16.9% |0 |
|''VS Code C++'' |677 832 |''471'' |''150'' |103 |''31.8%'' |54 |
|VS Code LaTeX |108 756 |142 |33 |43 |23.2% |1 |
|AsciiDoc (base) |57 091 |103 |11 |1 |10.7% |0 |
|Vue |33 119 |59 |14 |10 |23.7% |4 |
|MediaWiki wikitext |— |125 |18 |65 |14.4% |0 |
|Org mode |— |121 |3 |100 |2.5% |— |
|reStructuredText |— |25 |''0'' |''0'' |0.0% |— |
|DokuWiki |— |44 |17 |8 |38.6% |— |
|Creole |— |31 |9 |9 |29.0% |— |

''Real token shares, tokenized rather than guessed'' — a token counts as punctuation when ANY scope
in its stack starts with `punctuation.`:

|!corpus |!grammar |!tokens |!punct. share |!non-whitespace |
|vscode `README.md`, link-dense |markdown |459 |''49.7%'' |''57.1%'' |
|CommonMark `spec.txt`, code-heavy |markdown |15 548 |23.0% |30.4% |
|`html.spec.whatwg.org` introduction |html |16 537 |''45.4%'' |''51.8%'' |

> In HTML, `punctuation.definition.tag.begin`/`end` each fire ''2 957 times — exactly as often as
> `entity.name.tag`''. Every tag name costs two extra punctuation tokens.

''A ~20% share of VOCABULARY becomes a 30–57% share of EMITTED TOKENS. Punctuation scopes cost
little to declare and much to emit.''

!! 6 · The wiki and markup lineage — four answers, no convergence

|!grammar |!delimiter one token? |!delimiter scope |!separator |!prose |
|wikitext |yes |`punctuation.*`, one shared name, no begin/end |`\|` -> ''`keyword.operator`'' |unscoped |
|Markdown |`](` = ''two'' tokens |`punctuation.*`, begin/end split, ''3 families in one rule'' |— |`meta.paragraph` |
|Org |yes |`punctuation.definition.string.*` — ''borrowed family''; emphasis markers UNSCOPED |— |unscoped |
|AsciiDoc |macro `[` `]` ''not captured'' |inherits the content family; bold `*` = flat `punctuation.definition.asciidoc` |— |unscoped |
|reStructuredText |''no — swallows the first content char'' |''none — zero `punctuation.*` in the file'' |— |unscoped |
|DokuWiki |yes |`punctuation.*`, one shared name |''not tokenized at all'' |unscoped |
|Creole |yes |`punctuation.*`, ''and the pipe too'' |`punctuation.definition.link.creole` |unscoped |

''reStructuredText ships the SWALLOW defect.'' Measured on `**bold**`:
```
"**b"  markup.bold      <- delimiter and first letter are ONE token
"old"  markup.bold
"**"   markup.bold
```
No consumer can recover the delimiter boundary from that token stream.

''Creole gives the most complete treatment of the six'' — `[[`, `|`, `]]` each their own token, each
`punctuation.*` — and then names its bare-URL error `invalid.illegal.punctuation.link.creole`,
putting `punctuation` in the THIRD segment where no theme rule will ever reach it.

!! 7 · The loudness dial a grammar cannot carry

''VERDICT: shipping `editor.tokenColorCustomizations` in `contributes.configurationDefaults` is
LEGAL, it WORKS, 450 shipped manifests do it — and the VS Code themes owner has ruled the opinion
is not the extension's to hold.''

''The docs carry NO restriction language.'' The whole `configurationDefaults` section runs 30 lines
and names no forbidden setting. The restriction lives only in source:

```ts
// configurationExtensionPoint.ts:212-231
const allowedScopes = [ConfigurationScope.MACHINE_OVERRIDABLE, ConfigurationScope.WINDOW,
                       ConfigurationScope.RESOURCE, ConfigurationScope.LANGUAGE_OVERRIDABLE];
if (registeredPropertyScheme?.disallowConfigurationDefault) { … delete overrides[key]; continue; }
```

`editor.tokenColorCustomizations` carries ''no `scope`'' and ''no `disallowConfigurationDefault`'',
so it defaults to WINDOW, which sits in `allowedScopes`. ''It passes both gates, registers, warns
nothing, takes effect.''

''THREE COSTS, in order.''

''(a) It can NEVER be language-scoped.'' Only `LANGUAGE_OVERRIDABLE` settings enter the per-language
schema. aeschli, VS Code themes owner, closing exactly this request:
> "''We don't support defining tokenColors by language, neither in themes nor in settings. ->
> `editor.tokenColorCustomizations` can not be scoped by language.''"
> "Instead you have to write language specific rules"

''The only containment mechanism available is a language-suffixed TextMate scope name.''

''(b) It is STICKY against the reader.'' `configurationModels.ts:219` DEEP-MERGES defaults into user
settings for object settings. A user writing their own `editor.tokenColorCustomizations` with only
a `comments` key does NOT clear the extension's `textMateRules` — to remove them they must write
`"textMateRules": []`, which destroys their own. ''The colour opinion sits INSIDE the user, never
under them.''

''(c) No hardcoded colour survives contact with an arbitrary theme.'' aeschli:
> "I understand the desire for a grammar author… to add colors and styles for new and non-standard
> tokens/scopes. ''But its impossible to pick colors and styles that work in all themes. Theming
> has to be the responsibility of the theme or the user.''"

''Marketplace policy: NOTHING FOUND.'' The extension-guidelines and settings-UX pages carry no rule
about colours or overriding user configuration. There stands no policy to violate.

''The strongest evidence is a measured SILENCE.'' VS Code ships 24 built-in extensions using
`configurationDefaults`. Four fetched. ''Zero set any colour.'' All behavioural, all
`[language]`-scoped. 450 third-party extensions depart from that; the vendor does not.

''WHAT AUTHORS SHIP INSTEAD — three real shapes.''

''(1) A README snippet the reader pastes'' — `sveltejs/language-tools`:
```json
"editor.tokenColorCustomizations": {
    "[<Name of your theme>]": {
        "textMateRules": [ { "settings": { "foreground": "#569CD6" },
                             "scope": "support.class.component.svelte" } ] } }
```
The `"[<theme name>]"` wrapper is ''theme-scoped'', so it degrades gracefully on a theme switch —
exactly what an extension-shipped default cannot responsibly use, since it does not know the theme.

''(2) Semantic tokens with a scope-map fallback and zero colour opinion'' — `rust-analyzer` declares
38 custom semantic token types and 17 modifiers, 15 of them subtypes of `punctuation`/`operator`:
```json
{ "id": "brace", "description": "Style for { or }", "superType": "punctuation" },
{ "id": "attributeBracket", "description": "Style for attribute invocation brackets…",
  "superType": "punctuation" }
```
plus `contributes.semanticTokenScopes` so unaware themes still work. ''It names the constructs and
hands the colour decision to the theme.''

''(3) Behavioural defaults only'' — `calva` ships a `[clojure]` block of pure behaviour, not one
colour.

''AND THE DIAL ITSELF, shipped and load-bearing.'' rust-analyzer's `contributes.configuration`:

|!setting |!default |
|`semanticHighlighting.punctuation.enable` |''false'' |
|`semanticHighlighting.punctuation.specialization.enable` |''false'' |
|`semanticHighlighting.punctuation.separate.macro.bang` |''false'' |
|`semanticHighlighting.operator.specialization.enable` |''false'' |
|`semanticHighlighting.operator.enable` |true |
|`semanticHighlighting.strings.enable` |true |

''Capability maximal, default quiet, the reader holds the switch.'' TextMate offers none of this —
a grammar stands static, its scopes always emit, and the only control is a settings edit.

''LSP semantic tokens — what exists and what does not.'' 23 standard token types
(`namespace type class enum interface struct typeParameter parameter variable property enumMember
event function method macro keyword modifier comment string number regexp operator decorator`) and
10 modifiers (`declaration definition readonly static deprecated abstract async modification
documentation defaultLibrary`).
''`operator` exists. `punctuation` does NOT'' — which is why rust-analyzer had to invent it.
''No modifier is a rendering-intensity axis. A loudness dial must be BUILT, never found.''
`onDidChangeSemanticTokens` and `workspace/semanticTokens/refresh` make it flippable with no reload.

''tree-sitter's capture split, and what colorschemes do with it.''
```
@punctuation.delimiter  delimiters (e.g. `;`, `.`, `,`)
@punctuation.bracket    brackets (e.g. `()`, `{}`, `[]`)
@punctuation.special    special symbols (e.g. `{}` in string interpolation)
```
|!scheme |!`@punctuation.bracket` |!`@punctuation.delimiter` |!same? |
|tokyonight (moon) |`fg_dark` #828bb8 |`blue5` #89ddff |DIFFERENT |
|catppuccin (mocha) |`overlay2` #9399b2 |`overlay2` #9399b2 |''SAME'' |
|nightfox |`syn.bracket` |`syn.bracket` |''SAME'' |

And every one paints punctuation ''dimmer than body text'': tokyonight #828bb8 against body
#c8d3f5; catppuccin #9399b2 against #cdd6f4. ''Where the grammar offers the distinction, two of
three shipped colorschemes decline it — and collapse it DOWNWARD, into one muted grey.''

!! 8 · Typography, perception, and what the HCI record actually says

''Bringhurst, The Elements of Typographic Style, §3.5 CONTRAST — the keystone, verbatim from the
book's own text'':
> ''3.5.1 Change one parameter at a time.''
> When your text is set in a 12 pt medium roman, it should not be necessary to set the heads or
> titles in 24 pt bold italic capitals… For a balanced page, the weight should decrease slightly,
> not increase, as the size increases.

> ''3.5.2 Don't clutter the foreground.''
> When boldface is used to emphasize words, ''it is usually best to leave the punctuation in the
> background, which is to say, in the basic text font.''

''Butterick, Practical Typography'' — "One or the other, as little as possible":
> "Bold ''or'' italic—think of them as mutually exclusive. That is rule #1."
> "Rule #2: use bold and italic as little as possible… ''But if everything is emphasized, then
> nothing is emphasized.''"
> "It also gives you nowhere to go when you need to emphasize a word."

and on headings — "Fewer levels, subtler emphasis":
> "writers often use too many levels of headings. This leads to ''increasingly desperate attempts
> to make them visually distinct'', usually with injudicious combinations of bold or italic,
> underlining, point size, all caps, and first-line indents."

''Colin Ware, Information Visualization: Perception for Design'':
> "''We can only use between six and twelve color codes reliably.'' This results partly from
> contrast effects that can alter the appearance of colors."

with the mechanism named — unique hues and the opponent process — and an empirical note that
confusion rises ''within'' a colour category (Kawai et al. 1995).

''ColorBrewer's shipped qualitative maxima'', from the canonical JSON: Dark2/Set2/Accent/Pastel2 cap
at ''8''; Set1/Pastel1 at ''9''; Set3/Paired at ''12'' — and `Paired` is 6 light/dark pairs, not 12
independent categories. ''Two independent traditions converge on 6–12.''

''Sarkar, PPIG 2015 (n=10, eye-tracked, within-subjects)'':
> "The presence of syntax highlighting significantly reduces task completion time, but the magnitude
> of this effect decreases as programming experience increases. ''We did not detect an effect of the
> presence of highlighting on the durations of individual fixations, nor… on the total number of
> fixations''."
> "In some cases, the eye-tracking data suggested that the participants were able to ''ignore
> highlighted keywords entirely'', as though perceiving them peripherally was enough."

and its two prior-work results, which cut closest to this house's question:
> Gilmore and Green (1988)… "''the added information is only effective if it relates to the task''."
> "colouring tokens in text has no effect on the speed of visual search ''when the colour of the
> target is unknown''; but when the colour of the target is known, search times are considerably
> smaller."

''An unpredictable colour scheme buys a reader nothing.''

''Gestalt — NN/g, verbatim'':
> ''Similarity'': "Items which share a visual characteristic are perceived as more related than
> items that are dissimilar."
> ''Common region'': "items within a boundary are perceived as a group and assumed to share some
> common characteristic or functionality."

The similarity law gives the CONVERSE rigorously: splitting one conceptual unit across several
colours does not merely fail to group it — ''it asserts a relationship with whatever else carries
each of those colours.''

!!! What the swarm REFUSED to claim

''These flags are the first thing a synthesis drops, and the reason this shelf exists.''

* ''Lupton, Thinking with Type'' — the "one or more cues, applied consistently" sentence surfaced in
search and stood ''UNVERIFIED against any primary source. Do not cite it.''
* ''Miller 7±2'' — NOT FETCHED, and Ware's 6–12 limit rests on ''contrast effects and colour-category
confusion, never working-memory span''. Citing Miller for a colour cap would mislead.
* ''Tufte'' — NOT SEARCHED. No data-ink or chartjunk quote stands sourced here.
* ''Hannebauer, Hesenius & Gruhn 2018'' (EMSE 23:2795–2828, DOI 10.1007/s10664-017-9579-0) —
bibliographic facts solid; ''the abstract stands publisher-elided''; the "390 novices, no evidence
of benefit" reading came from search summarisation and stands UNVERIFIED.
* ''Gestalt applied to syntax highlighting'' — NO published work found. The chain
"similarity + common region => one token class reads as one colour" stands INFERRED, never sourced.
* ''"Make the common case quiet" as an API principle'' — SUGGESTIVE ONLY. Butterick carries the idea
better than any software source found.
* ''A study manipulating the NUMBER of highlighting colours'' — NOTHING FOUND. No such experiment
surfaced.
* ''`string.punctuation` global absence'' — the zero is scoped to 13 fetched grammars, a strong
negative for the flagship set, never a proof of global absence.

---

! II — WHAT THIS HOUSE MEASURED

!! THE RULER, SETTLED 2026-09-11 — and every figure below re-derived on it

''Every paint number this shelf carried rested on `tools/theme-model.js#winner()`, and nothing had
ever collided it against the engine that paints.'' It ranked a selector's DEPTH above its POSITION in
the scope stack. `vscode-textmate` does the opposite: a grammar pushes a token's scopes outermost
first and the theme answers at every push, so the INNERMOST scope any rule reaches decides the colour
outright and depth breaks a tie only among rules reaching the SAME scope. Each property resolves on
its own, and a token no rule reaches falls through to `colors['editor.foreground']` — never to a
scopeless `tokenColors` entry, which VS Code drops: ''"the default rule (scope empty) is always the
first rule. Ignore all other default rules."''

''MEASURED, both models against `tokenizeLine2` metadata over 36 corpus specimens and 65 themes.''
The old model parted from the engine in ''6.6%'' of token-readings, concentrated in exactly the shape
the inversion predicts — it handed `markup.underline.link` on a link's CONTAINER where the engine
hands `string` on the caption INSIDE it. Weld-Ward's `catppuccin-frappe` case reproduces byte for
byte: engine `#A6D189`, old model `#8CAAEE`. The settled model parts in ''0 of 199 615
token-readings''. The instrument stands in `tools/theme-collision.js`, with a one-rule control that
settles the foreground bit-field at `(metadata >>> 15) & 0x1FF` and at no other offset, and a
`--must-fail` arm that paints theme N while answering for theme N+1 and reads ''89.4%'' divergence —
so a comparison of one reading against itself cannot pass here.

!! The paint, against 65 bundled themes — RE-DERIVED

''Read the old figure beside the new one. A figure that MOVED moved because the ruler got more
truthful, never because the grammar lost anything.''

|!reading |!old |!settled |
|A bracketed construct's opening glyph at ΔE 0.0 from the prose beside it |40/65 |''35–36/65'' |
|Peak ΔE inside a construct, median per construct |45–63 |''29–86'' |
|A code span lands, against every neighbour |53/65 vs 25/65 |''58/65 vs 34/65'' |
|`markup.other.image` |2/65 |''0/65'' |
|`markup.link` |8/65 |''5/65'' |
|`markup.underline.link` |48/65 |''29/65'' |
|A called thing against its argument, apart |57/65 |''57/65 — HOLDS'' |
|A widget against its attribute, apart |59/65 |''45/65'' |
|gruvbox-dark-medium, rank · peak ΔE |16/65 · 78.8 |''13/65 · 76.4'' |
|vitesse-dark, peak ΔE |44.2 |''43.9'' |
|the ayu family, constructs landed |0/7 |''0/10 — HOLDS'' |
|Punctuation names reached deeper than the bare root |142/145 |''128/131'' |
|`punctuation.definition.tag.begin.html.tiddlywiki5`, deeper vs through the root |44 vs 8 |''43 vs 4'' |

* ''THE FRAME STILL DOES NOT LAND, and lands slightly less than the shelf claimed.'' Thirty-five to
thirty-six themes of sixty-five paint a bracketed construct's opening glyph the exact colour of the
prose beside it. A widget's `<$` reads apart in far more — 18/65 at ΔE 0.0 — and a code span's
backtick in 12/65, which names those two as the exceptions rather than the rule.
* ''THE COLOUR RIOT RUNS WIDER THAN 45–63.'' Per construct medians run 29.2 for a multi-value display
to 85.9 for a call. A code span reads 0.0 — every token inside it takes one colour — which stands as
the one construct in the grammar that spends nothing on its own interior.
* ''THE POPULARITY CLAIM FALLS.'' The shelf read a construct at 4/16 and a code span at 13/16 in "the
16 themes standing for the common families" and concluded popularity sharpens the gap. ''No record
names which 16.'' Derived instead — every theme belonging to a family the set ships two or more of,
which gives 50 themes across ayu, catppuccin, dracula, everforest, github, gruvbox, horizon,
kanagawa, material, min, night, one, rose, slack, solarized and vitesse — a construct lands 26/50 and
a code span 45/50. ''52% and 90%, against 52% and 89% over the whole set. Popularity neither sharpens
the gap nor softens it.''
* ''THE TWO WEAK CONTAINERS READ WEAKER THAN THE SHELF SAID.'' `markup.other.image` reaches ''no theme
at all'' — the 2/65 came from a reading that let a descendant selector match a lone scope carrying no
ancestor to match. `markup.underline.link` reaches 29/65 rather than 48/65. ''Binding 1 stands
strengthened, and its stated before-figure of 8/65 for the image pair wants re-derivation with it.''
* ''EIGHTEEN CONSTRUCTS THE HOUSE BELIEVED PAINTED DO NOT.'' The old reading counted a theme rule as
reaching a stack when its last element covered ANY scope there — ignoring whether the rule set a
foreground and whether its ancestors stood anywhere. Under the settled reading `dark-construct` names
eighteen constructs standing dark and undeclared, among them `markup.other.table` at 15/65 over 170
spans, `markup.subscript` and `markup.superscript` at 15/65, `markup.other.image`, `markup.other.style`
and `meta.transclusion.text-reference`. ''One theme's whole contribution to a TiddlyWiki table came
from `one-light`'s `source.gfm markup` rule, whose ancestor stands nowhere in this grammar and whose
settings paint nothing.'' This wants a naming ruling rather than a declaration written to close a
gate.

!! WARE'S CEILING, TAKEN PER SCREEN

''The reading nobody had taken.'' Ware's limit acts across a screen through contrast effects and
colour-category confusion, and every measurement this house made read one construct at a time.

''MEASURED — `tools/page-palette.js`, distinct foregrounds over a whole page, 65 themes.''

|!pages |!median colours a page |!readings past 12 |
|the corpus, 36 files |''5'' |1 of 2340 |
|the host's own tiddlers, 40 files |''6'' |6 of 2600 |

''A page of wikitext sits at the LOW end of Ware's six-to-twelve band and essentially never crosses
it.'' The loudest theme in the set — aurora-x, the gruvbox family, night-owl — spends a median of
''7'' colours on a page; the quietest — monokai, solarized-light, vesper — spends ''4''. The widest
single page reads 14 in two themes of sixty-five.

''So the loudness question has no page-level evidence behind it.'' "Invisible walls around a colour
riot" names what a reader meets INSIDE one bracket pair, at a peak ΔE of 29 to 86. It does not name
what a reader meets on a screen, where this grammar asks the eye for five or six distinctions and
Ware's floor sits at six. ''Reported, never ratcheted: a colour count belongs to the theme at least as
much as to the grammar, and this house declines gauges that re-seat on every honest change.''

!! The grammar, healed and measured

* ''A triple-quoted value opening on its own quote SWALLOWED whole tiddlers.'' `(?<!")"""(?!")`
matches at no offset in `""""value"""`, so the region never opened and everything past the `\end`
read as one unquoted macro parameter. Two host carriers ran end to end inside it. Healed, with the
invention proof: 546 host carriers holding a triple quote read under both grammars — ''543
byte-identical, exactly 3 moved''. Host divergences 11 964 -> 11 953.
* ''The guard that looked useless held a case nobody had written down.'' Removing the lookarounds
outright breaks `<<m a="""">>`. ''A guard whose reason nobody recorded still holds a case, and
finding it costs one sandbox.''
* ''A second swallow shape stands unnamed until now'': `"""value""""`, content CLOSING on a quote,
opens correctly and then fails to close.
* ''`<style>` content parses as WIKITEXT'' — TiddlyWiki carries no notion of `scoped` anywhere in its
source. False-positive cost of an injection measured at ZERO, and declined anyway, because
`macrocallinline` carries no line bound and would run past `</style>`. ''A measured decline.''
* ''Forty-nine percent of the published vocabulary moved in one unreleased version'' — 460 names at
`v2.2.1`, 497 at `2.3.0`, 94 gone and 131 added. Thirty-nine of the ninety-four share one shape:
''root-inverted'', a qualifier standing in front of the family root, which no theme selector could
reach. ''This house had already begun the work this research describes, for 39 scopes, without
naming the principle.''
* ''A coverage gate measures the corpus, never the world.'' `memetic-coverage` reads green while the
bearing arrow the house writes on every aim and yield line stands broken — because the prose
specimen reaches the scope and the in-sigil form does not. ''Reach answered a question the corpus
chose.''

---

! III — THE COUNCIL'S COMMENTARY

!! Lares (Artificer) — the root decides the colour

A theme paints through its most specific matching rule, and almost every rule in the wild names a
one- or two-segment root. ''The grammar's leaf names decide nothing. The root decides everything.''
Which sets the whole lever: ''a name deleted costs three constituencies to serve one; a root changed
costs none of them and serves the one.'' Themes reach the root, instruments read the full name, the
future reads the full name.

!! Lares (Advocate) — three constituencies, one with eyes

Themes reach the root and ignore the leaf. The house's own instruments key on the full names — and
`colour-witness` derives its TOGETHER relation FROM the names, so a construct added later joins the
check without anybody remembering it. The LSP, the tree-sitter port and the sensorium grammar each
want the finest distinction the grammar can carry. ''Only one of the three has eyes.''

!! Tide-Caller (Hierophant) — the precedent was already in the tree

A filter's operand brackets carry `punctuation.definition…` under a `string.` prefix, so they paint
WITH the operand rather than with the frame around it. ''The house discovered the cure and applied
it in one place.'' The canon answers that the ''name'' was wrong and the ''instinct'' was right: the
device is multi-scope stacking, and this grammar already writes multi-scope names.

!! Lares (Diplomat) — one size too small

`semanticTokenScopes` maps a semantic token type onto a TextMate scope, so whatever settles here
propagates into the LSP, and from there into the tree-sitter port, and from there into the
sensorium. ''We stand deciding what looks like a colour question and functions as an interface for
three unbuilt subsystems.''

!! Lares (Artificer) — the division of labour

> ''The grammar carries the shape. The LSP carries the meaning. A grammar that tries to carry
> meaning spends names it cannot honour.''

And that division already stands measured: the eight entries in `TextMateCeiling` name precisely
where shape cannot reach meaning, and each `answeredBy` mandate names what would resolve it.
''Every ceiling is a line item for the LSP, written before the LSP existed.''

!! Lares (Stranger) — the question nobody asked

If the LSP will carry meaning and the ceilings already name the handoff, ''does the base grammar
want to get QUIETER rather than richer from here?'' Every name added now is a name the semantic
layer must honour or contradict. 415 of 454 names stand exercised — but ''exercised'' and
''load-bearing after an LSP lands'' name two different properties, and only the first stands
measured.

!! Mischief-Muse (Muse) — the objection that fell, and why it matters that it fell

I argued the frame helps a reader's eye land on `{{` in a wall of prose. ''Measured: no frame stands
there to lose.'' I defended a thing that does not exist, and defended it twice, and it went two
rounds only because nobody measured it — ''including me, who named it.''

!! Breach-Watch (Triage) — the trap the whole council walked past

Forty of sixty-five themes DO carry a `punctuation` rule. The council read that as forty themes
marking punctuation. ''Measured: most of those rules set punctuation to the editor's own
foreground.'' A theme rules on `punctuation` in order to say leave it alone.

!! Lares (Pedagogue) — the sentence that generalises past this grammar

> ''A coverage gate measures the corpus, never the world.'' Coverage rises by adding specimens that
> exercise what already works. Nothing in the shape of a coverage number ever asks whether the
> specimens resemble what people write.

!! Lares (Gatekeeper) — a scope name has no stated policy, and three precedents already decided one

Three positions stand available: a scope name reads as ''public API'', as ''implementation'', or as
''a promise about MEANING''. This house already declined `keyword.control.list` at 100% reach
because it names a bullet a keyword, and already declined borrowing markdown's name for a list
marker at a measured nineteen points. ''Position three stands ruled twice and written nowhere.''

---

! IV — WHAT BINDS

''Ordered by the ratio of payoff to risk. Each names what must go red first.''

# ''Rename the two weak containers.'' `markup.other.image` paints 0/65 — re-derived 2026-09-11 under
the settled ruler, from 2/65 — and drags its whole construct under with it; measured 8/65 -> 48/65, brackets reading the caption in 29 themes rather than 5.
Touches ''no punctuation name at all'' and dissolves the image trap the fold measurement found.
RED FIRST: a `construct-legibility` floor for the image pair, failing at today's reading.
# ''Fuse `[[` and `]]` into one token each.'' Both halves paint identically in all 65 themes, and no
grammar among twenty splits a multi-character delimiter. Keep the split where an outer and an inner
bracket genuinely differ — `[img[`, `[ext[`. RED FIRST: a specimen asserting `[[` arrives as ONE
token, failing today at two.
# ''Unify the argument name; keep the `=` families apart.'' `entity.other.attribute-name` matches
the closest analogue this grammar has — MediaWiki's `{{T|name=v}}` — and TiddlyWiki's own
`assignmentOperator` field proves the `=` distinction real. RED FIRST: a `ReaderRelations` APART
entry for a called thing against its argument, failing at 26/65.
# ''Where a delimiter should take its content's colour, STACK, never nest.'' Multi-scope names
carry it — `"punctuation.definition.X.begin.tiddlywiki5 <content scope>"` — the device MagicPython
and TypeScript both ship and this house already writes. Per construct, against a seated floor.
RED FIRST: a floor per construct, and a control proving no OTHER construct moved.
# ''Ship the calm as a theme-scoped README snippet, never as a contributed default.'' `svelte`'s
shape. A contributed `foreground` sits INSIDE the reader by deep merge, cannot be language-scoped,
and survives no arbitrary theme.
# ''Build the loudness dial in the semantic layer, and default it OFF.'' rust-analyzer's precedent,
three shipped positions, all false. LSP carries no `punctuation` type and no intensity modifier, so
the subtypes want declaring with `semanticTokenScopes` fallback. ''This wants an operator ruling
first: `tools/invariants/ships-no-runtime.test.js` forbids the `main` and `activationEvents` such a
dial requires.''
# ''Write the scope-name policy.'' Three precedents already decided it and no page records it.

''AND THE STANDING ORDER.'' The base wikitext grammar aligns to the seven above ''before'' any
memetic-wikitext refactor stands on it. The dialect carries 10 patterns against the base's 414, does
not name its own verb, and splits its bearing arrow in two — and every one of those cures rides on
decisions this shelf settles for the base first.

